/* ---------- screens ---------- */
const HOW = `<ul>
<li>Tap the + and − buttons, pinch with two fingers or use the mouse wheel to zoom in. While zoomed in, drag with one finger, or use WASD or the arrow keys, to look around. On a tablet or phone, drag sideways on empty ground to look along the map, and double-tap to come back.</li>
<li>Creeps walk the shortest route from the portal to your keep. Every tower blocks that route, so arrange them into a long maze.</li>
<li>You can't seal the path completely.</li>
<li>With the keep's towers, every wave is a dungeon on the march, led by someone with a rule of their own: the info pane shows who leads the next one and how to beat them, and a fallen leader may drop a relic. A force's march has no leaders: a boss comes every 5th wave and elites every 3rd, and the info pane shows what each boss does.</li>
<li>You start with the steel schools (Arrow, Cannon and Glaive) and one more school chosen at random for each run. The boss of every 5th wave carries a School Key, and a key opens any school you choose: its towers and its talents. Tap a locked card in the build bar to use it. Pick the school your waves (and your relics) call for.</li>
<li>Glaive Throwers hurl a glaive that bounces from foe to foe, a little weaker with every bounce. Great on a crowd, weaker on a lone boss.</li>
<li>Barricades are cheap maze walls. Tap one later to turn it into a tower.</li>
<li>Double-tap a tower to select every tower of the same type and tier, then upgrade them all at once.</li>
<li>Press Space, or tap the pause button above the zoom buttons, to freeze the battle. You can still build and upgrade while it's paused. Enter sounds the horn.</li>
<li>Flyers ignore the maze and cut straight down the middle. Cannons can't hit them.</li>
<li>Anything built since the last wave sells for a full refund.</li>
<li>Sound the horn early to earn bonus gold. During a wave, the horn sends the next wave on top of it for even more.</li>
<li>Elemental creeps shrug off their own element: frost wolves ignore Frost, magma beetles ignore Flame, and so on. Check the wave panel for immunities and weaknesses.</li>
<li>Towers climb to tier VI, but tiers IV to VI must be unlocked first. Tap your keep to upgrade it: a Castle opens tier IV, a Fortress tier V and a Citadel tier VI. Each upgrade costs a lot of gold, takes effect at once and only becomes available after a certain wave.</li>
<li>Tiers IV to VI hit far harder. Going to tier IV, each tower chooses one of two paths for itself (a Marksman or a Skirmisher, say); its tier V and VI perks follow that path. Tier V also opens a third socket.</li>
<li>Falling behind? Below half your lives your keep rallies: kills pay 25% more gold and towers hit 15% harder (the lives counter glows red). After any wave that lets foes through, the realm sends war aid: gold for every foe that got past.</li>
<li>The Shop (the coin pouch) sells finished items for your towers, and bigger artifacts worth saving for. About a third have an ability you tap from the tower's buttons. Items and relics share a tower's sockets. Anything bought sells back in full until the next wave starts, and at half after that.</li>
<li>Fast creeps shrug off slows and stuns. Casters heal, shield, silence towers or raise the dead, so kill them first. Fifty waves stand between you and victory.</li>
<li>Creeps fight as a warband. Warcallers whip their neighbours into a frenzy, Shieldbearers block shots at the allies behind them, Menders heal, Shamans wash off your Frost, Venom and curses, and Torchbearers thaw frozen allies. Pack Alphas speed up their wolves, and the pack panics when the alpha falls. Burrowers dive underground when focused, where only Quake Totems reach them. Storm Wisps feed on lightning.</li>
<li>The fallen leave corpses. Necromancers raise them and Carrion Beasts eat them to grow, but burned or shattered corpses are no use to either. Tap any creep to read what it does.</li>
<li>From wave 30, every 10th wave brings siege troops. They ignore your maze and march straight for the gate, smashing whatever tower or barricade stands in their way. A wrecked tower is an open breach and stops firing. Nothing repairs itself: select a damaged tower to pay for repairs.</li>
<li>From wave 3 on, every wave brings elites, ringed in gold. They are tougher, and now and then one drops a relic. Relics come in five rarities: common, uncommon, rare, epic and legendary. Socket one into a tower (fully upgraded towers take two) to give it bonuses. Legendary relics go further and change how a tower works: borrowed elements, overkill that spills over, attacks that become pulses, kills that reset the attack. Each one suggests what to pair it with. Selling a tower returns its relics.</li>
<li>A force commander's units each start with one spell, their innate one. Their other two spells unlock in the unit's talent tree: the round spell talents, on rows II to V (each unit's tree places them its own way). The deeper a spell sits, the stronger it is: harder hits or a longer effect, for a little more mana. Each needs the talent straight above it at full rank, and the talents that improve a spell sit below it. Once a spell is unlocked, every unit of that kind knows it at once, and so does every one you build later. Resetting the tree makes them forget it. Most other talents in a force's trees reshape its spells: cheaper, quicker, wider, or harder against foes that are held, frozen, burning or marked.</li>
<li>With the keep's towers, every wave you clear earns a talent point. A force commander earns them by experience instead: slain foes fill the XP bar beside the wave counter (bosses and elites the most, foes that get through nothing), and every level pays a talent point, about one a wave. Every talent is a single point with a big effect. Spend them under Talents to reshape how a tower type fights; the card shows exactly what a talent changes before you take it. Rows open in every tree as you earn points (one row every two), so you can mix schools freely. Most talents come with a drawback, so choose carefully.</li>
<li>Reactions happen on their own when two schools meet on a foe: Frost and Flame Melt, lightning on a chilled foe Superconducts, Flame on a poisoned foe goes Volatile, quakes and cannons Crush frozen foes, quakes on a burning foe set off an Eruption. Arcane bolts hit harder for every effect on a foe, and arrows and glaives hit stunned foes harder. With 2 points in two trees you can learn their Fusion talent, which supercharges a reaction.</li>
<li>Relics are rare. Bosses (and the keep's dungeon leaders) sometimes drop one, elites now and then, and any other foe once in a long while. The first waves drop rare (blue) relics; epics start turning up from wave 6, and legendaries, rarer still, from wave 11, growing more likely as the run goes on. Many of them are school relics: one legendary for each school that empowers every tower of that school you own, more for every one you build. A Storm relic early is a good reason to spend your next School Key on Storm.</li>
<li>Every wave is weak to its dungeon's counter schools (×1.75 damage) and resists two others (×0.7), shown as icons on the wave card. Read the next wave and build for it.</li>
<li>Clear a wave without a leak to build a Flawless streak: +10% gold from kills and wave bonuses per clean wave, up to +50%. One leak resets it.</li>
<li>Every row of a talent tree is a choice of two ways the tower can work, so each tree is five decisions. Learn one and the other is locked; reset the tree between waves to choose again.</li>
<li>Gold you don't spend earns interest when a wave is cleared, and the more you hold the higher the rate: nothing under 100 gold, 5% at 100, then +1% for every further 100, up to 12% at 800 (each payout is capped at 20 + 8 per wave). The green number beside your gold is what you will earn. Saving pays, but towers win waves.</li>
<li>Paths (in Talents) are six short tracks, each built around one system: reactions and fusions, relics, talents, towers and the keep, the maze, and gold. You get a path point at the start and one for every boss beaten (waves 5, 10, 15 ...). Spend each on the next rank of any path; ten points over a full game finish two paths.</li>
<li>Some relics are made for one tower (marked with a blue dot, and they only fit that tower). Signature relics are legendaries forged for one tower type. Tomes of Mastery teach a tower one capstone of its own tree on top of the capstone you chose. Catalysts make one reaction stronger whenever the tower holding them sets it off. These drops lean toward the towers you have on the field.</li>
<li>Base relics belong to sets. Socket two or three different pieces of a set, in any towers they fit, and the towers holding them get the set bonus. Every tower type also has a rare and an epic relic of its own, which only work in that tower; most of them pair up, or join pieces for other towers, in sets.</li>
<li>The War Room (the book in the top bar, or J) shows a battle report after every wave: which towers did the work and how much came from direct hits, burns and poison, reactions and fusions, and relic powers. It also names your build, keeps your combo journal across runs and scouts the waves ahead.</li>
<li>Every wave has towers that answer it and towers it shrugs off: armor blunts Arrow, Cannon and Earth, magic resistance blunts Frost, Flame and Storm, and elementals shrug off some of their own element (it lands at 60%). Nothing is immune: the wrong tower only makes a fight harder. The wave panel, the wave banner and Scouting in the War Room say which towers are strong or weak against what is coming, and a tower you are about to build shows how it fares against the next wave.</li>
<li>While placing or selecting a tower, the towers it reacts with, fuses with or is blessed by glow in the reaction's colour, and the info panel lists every synergy.</li>
<li>Every build has a code (War Room, Build). Paste someone else's to load it as a plan: planned talents are marked, and new talent points are spent on it for you.</li>
<li>Challenges on the title screen restrict a run (three schools only, no barricades, one life ...) and keep their own best wave.</li></ul>`;
